th the increased workload on the heart due to a VSD, leading to pulmonary congestion and heart failure. The high-pitched holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border is a classic sign of VSD, indicating turbulent blood flow from the left ventricle to the right ventricle. The absence of cyanosis further supports the diagnosis of a left-to-right shunt, which is typical in VSDs. Among the given options, a 22q11 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome) is the most plausible cause, as it is associated with a high incidence of VSDs and can present with similar symptoms. This genetic syndrome also aligns with the infant's presentation, making it the most likely underlying cause of the described symptoms and physical findings.''' \n"], "raw_output": "Option: A"}
