tant to consider other potential pathogens such as Enterobacteriaceae (e.g., Klebsiella pneumoniae) and less commonly, anaerobic bacteria. These should be included in the differential diagnosis and empirical antibiotic coverage should be broad enough to cover these possibilities. The patient should also be evaluated for underlying conditions that may predispose to these infections, such as diabetes or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and appropriate management of these conditions should be initiated or optimized. Regular neurological assessments should be conducted to monitor for any changes in the patient's neurological status.]''' \n"], "raw_output": "Option: B"}
